[PAGE title="@explorer/Welcome" hide toc="false" contentX="center" maxWidth="880px"]

	# sdocs

	A lightweight documentation tool for Svelte 5 components.

	```bash
	npm install -D sdocs
	```

	<div style="display: flex; gap: 12px; margin: 8px 0 40px;">
		<LinkButton href="/explorer/getting-started">Get started</LinkButton>
		<LinkButton href="/explorer/overview" variant="secondary">Documentation</LinkButton>
	</div>

	## One small file per component

	Drop a `.sdoc` file next to a component and it appears in an interactive
	explorer: live preview, controls generated from `args`, and a prop table
	extracted from the component itself. No stories API to learn — a doc file
	*is* a Svelte file, with snippets for every state you want to show.

	```sdoc
	<script lang="ts">
		import Button from './Button.svelte';
	</script>

	[SHOWCASE title="@components/Button" description="A flexible button."]

		[preview component={Button} args={{ label: 'Click me' }}]
			<Button {...args} />
		[/preview]

		[example title="Sizes"]
			<Button label="Small" size="sm" />
			<Button label="Large" size="lg" />
		[/example]

	[/SHOWCASE]
	```

	## The three faces of sdocs

	<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin: 16px 0;">
		<Card href="/explorer/overview" title="Explorer" linkText="Explore the docs">
			The interactive component browser built from your .sdoc files — live
			previews, prop controls, theming, and search.
		</Card>
		<Card href="/cli/overview" title="CLI" linkText="Command reference">
			npx sdocs run starts the Explorer in any project with zero install, and
			sdocs build ships it as a static site.
		</Card>
		<Card href="/extension/overview" title="VS Code Extension" linkText="Extension guide">
			Full Svelte IntelliSense in .sdoc files, plus a Projects view that runs
			and shows your docs inside the editor.
		</Card>
	</div>

